  "summary": "Large communal rammed-earth buildings, circular or square in shape, housing multiple families.",
  "description": "Massive circular or square rammed-earth structures with thick walls and interior wooden frameworks designed for communal living and defense. Built by Hakka and Minnan peoples to protect against bandits and natural disasters while maintaining extended family connections.",
  "culturalContext": "Fujian tulou — rammed-earth compounds of the Hakka and other communities — grew between the 15th and 20th centuries in southwest Fujian's hill country, housing whole clans behind walls up to five storeys thick for defense against banditry. One courtyard, one gate, concentric rings of rooms around ancestral halls; UNESCO listed 46 tulou in 2008, and village clusters like Yongding and Nanjing keep them in daily use.",
